WEBVTT

00:00.400 --> 00:17.506
Duplication percentage tells you how much analysed source is covered by reported clones. It does not tell you how accurate the detector is. A false positive can inflate the percentage, and a missed clone can reduce it. Correct arithmetic does not establish correct detection.

00:18.356 --> 00:38.890
Analysed lines include physical source lines, including blanks and comments. Duplicated lines are the union of visible clone ranges within each file, so overlaps count once. Hidden occurrences do not enter the numerator, while their analysed files remain in the denominator. Excluded files leave analysis entirely.

00:39.740 --> 01:00.293
Accuracy checks use judged source pairs pinned to repository commits. Clearly in identifies a known clone; clearly out identifies a pair that should not be reported. Unclear cases assert neither verdict. Range coverage separately asks whether the report covers the whole judged copy rather than just a small fragment.

01:01.143 --> 01:20.613
The gate fails when the full-precision percentage is greater than its ceiling. Equality passes, even if display rounding makes nearby values look identical. Read the report scope and the measured build together. Corpus results apply to judged examples; they do not guarantee perfect precision or recall everywhere.
